🇾🇪Yemen’s Houthi rebels are making a concerted drive to take full control of the country’s Red Sea coast, claiming control of the key port of Mocha, in the most serious wave of fighting with the 🇸🇦Saudi-backed government in years.

Saudi Arabia's last oil route is in play. Seizure of the coast would be a strategic disaster for Saudi Arabia and 🇺🇸US, taking Iran and its proxies closer to full control of two chokepoints for western shipping on opposite sides of the Arabian peninsula: the Bab al-Mandeb at the southern end of the Red Sea and the strait of Hormuz in the Gulf.

The conflict data monitor Acled estimated there were 276 fatalities over the 5 day period alone. The fighting has continued, with the Houthis claiming control of Mocha in Taiz province, as well as the inland city of Hays and the Khalid ibn al-Walid camp, the largest military base in the region. One Houthi-linked estimate suggested rebel forces had gained control of more than 6,000 km2 (2,320 square miles) of land.

The role of US in this side of the conflict remains undefined, as Trump’s Administration and Pentagon have so far focused their political and military efforts on dealing with the Persian Gulf region in general, and the Hormuz closure in particular. 

The role of 🇦🇪UAE, who have funded some of the Yemen separatists - especially the so-called Southern Transitional Council - is also unclear. 

What’s the most likely scenario in the Yemen conflict:

A) Opening of the 2nd front in the Iran war;

B) US military intervention in Yemen, righting the Houthis alongside Saudi Arabia;

C) US political intervention to lift restrictions on 🇮🇷Iran / blockade of Hormuz, thereby reducing hostilities in Yemen;

D) Saudi Arabia activating the recent Mecca pact with 🇹🇷Turkey and 🇵🇰Pakistan to fight together in Yemen; 

E) Other?

